---
description: createHistory keeps an undo and redo timeline for commands or state snapshots, folds a drag or a burst of typing into one step, and historyKeys binds it to the keyboard.
---

<script>
</script>

`createHistory` keeps a timeline of steps. Each step is a pair of functions, so
one history serves edits you describe as commands and edits you capture as
whole states. `historyKeys` is an action that binds Command or Control + Z to
it, and decides what happens when focus is in a text field that has an undo of
its own. Neither touches the DOM, so both are safe to import on the server.

```js
import { createHistory, historyKeys } from "carbon-components-svelte";

const history = createHistory({ limit: 100, coalesceMs: 500 });
const { canUndo, canRedo, undoLabel } = history;
```

```svelte
<div use:historyKeys={history}>
  <button disabled={!$canUndo} on:click={() => history.undo()}>
    Undo {$undoLabel}
  </button>
</div>
```

## Commands

Describe a change as a pair of functions and record it with `run`, which calls
`do` and then keeps the step. Use `push` when the change has already happened,
such as one made by a drag. A new step after an undo drops the steps that were
undone. The `label` names the step, so a button can read "Undo move Ada up".

Make `do` and `undo` set absolute values, as the example does: the list after
the change, and the list before it. A step that holds its own copy of both
sides stays correct however many steps come before it.

<FileSource src="/framed/History/HistoryCommands" />

## Snapshots

When the state is a value you can keep, such as a form's values, record each
state after a change with `snapshots`. Undo applies the state before the step
and redo applies the state after it. Treat states as immutable and build each
one from the last with a spread, so a step shares everything that did not
change and costs only what moved. Recording a state equal to the current one
adds nothing (compared with `Object.is`, or the `equals` you pass), which is
also why a reactive statement that records after every update does not record
an undo again. A control that echoes an event when you set its value from
script, such as the `check` event of `Checkbox`, hands you a new object with
the same fields: compare before you record, as the example does.

Typing is the case for `key`. Steps with the same key that arrive within
`coalesceMs` fold into one, so a burst of typing in a field is one step and a
pause starts a new one, the way the browser's own undo groups it. Choose how
Command or Control + Z behaves inside the text fields with the selector.

<FileSource src="/framed/History/HistorySnapshots" />

### Large states

`record` takes the state after the change, so building it costs time and memory
for every keystroke. For a state too large for that, such as a rich text
document held as a string, set `capture` and call `checkpoint` before the
change instead. It reads the state only when a step starts, so a folded
keystroke costs a timestamp check, and redo reads the state again when you
undo.

```js
const doc = history.snapshots({
  initial: editor.innerHTML,
  capture: () => editor.innerHTML,
  apply: (html) => (editor.innerHTML = html),
  cost: (html) => html.length,
});

editor.addEventListener("beforeinput", () =>
  doc.checkpoint({ key: "typing", label: "typing" }),
);
```

## Gestures

A drag sends dozens of changes and should undo as one. Two tools cover it.

| Tool | Use it when | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `key` on each step | The steps are absolute (set the opacity to 40) and arrive in a burst. | A folded step keeps the first `undo` and the last `do`, so a million steps hold one step's memory. |
| `begin()` and `commit()` | The steps are relative (move by 4), or you want the drag to end the step instead of a pause. | A group composes every step in order, and undoes them in reverse. |

`cancel()` ends the group and undoes what it recorded, with nothing left to
redo. Use it for Escape during a drag. While a group is open, `undo()` and
`redo()` do nothing, so a key pressed mid-drag cannot take back a step the
drag depends on. `transaction(label, fn)` wraps the three calls: it commits
when `fn` returns and cancels when it throws.

Drag the slider, then undo: the whole drag comes back as one step. Press
<DocKbd label="Escape" /> during a drag to put the value back.

<FileSource src="/framed/History/HistoryGesture" />

## Keyboard

`historyKeys` listens for keydown inside the element it is on, so a page with
more than one history gives each its own container. A handled key calls
`preventDefault`, so the browser's undo stays out of the way.

The keys reach the element only while focus is inside it, and focus does not
always stay. Removing or moving the focused element drops focus to the page,
and Safari does not focus a checkbox or a button when it is clicked. Either
way the next Command + Z goes nowhere. Keep focus in the container, as the
commands example does after a delete, or set `scope: "document"` to listen to
every key on the page. Use the document for the one history that owns the
page, as the snapshots example does.

| Platform | Undo | Redo |
| --- | --- | --- |
| macOS and iOS | <DocKbd label="Command" />+<DocKbd label="Z" /> | <DocKbd label="Command" />+<DocKbd label="Shift" />+<DocKbd label="Z" /> |
| Windows and Linux | <DocKbd label="Control" />+<DocKbd label="Z" /> | <DocKbd label="Control" />+<DocKbd label="Shift" />+<DocKbd label="Z" /> or <DocKbd label="Control" />+<DocKbd label="Y" /> |

Control + Y is not bound on macOS, where text fields use it to paste the last
text cut with Control + K. A key pressed during an IME composition, or one
another handler already prevented, is left alone. A held key repeats.

## Text fields

A text field has an undo stack of its own, and Command or Control + Z there
belongs to it unless you say otherwise. The `fields` option decides.

| `fields` | Command or Control + Z in a text field | Use it when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `"native"` (default) | The browser undoes its own typing. The history is untouched. | The field is a plain input and the history tracks other things. |
| `"fallthrough"` | The browser tries first. If it had nothing to undo, the history undoes a step. | The history records the field only when the user commits it, on blur or on Enter. |
| `"app"` | The history undoes a step. The browser's undo never runs. | The history records every edit in the field, as the snapshots example does. |

Outside a text field, which includes a read-only field, a checkbox, a select
and a button, the history always handles the key.

| Do | Why |
| --- | --- |
| Keep the default `"native"` when the history does not record typing. | The browser undoes typing correctly. Taking the key would leave typed text nobody can undo. |
| Use `"app"` when the history records typing, and write edits through `value` and an `input` event. | Two undo stacks over one field disagree: the browser undoes text the history still counts. |
| Keep scripted edits to a field you leave to the browser in `execCommand("insertText")`. | Setting `value` or calling `setRangeText` clears the browser's undo stack for that field. |
| Do not split one form between the two. | In Chromium and WebKit the browser keeps one undo stack for the whole page, so Command + Z in an untouched field can undo typing in another. |

## Limits

`limit` keeps the newest steps (100 by default). Set `maxCost` and give steps a
`cost`, such as a string's length, to cap memory when states differ in size;
the newest step always stays. Steps hold what they close over, so a history of
snapshots holds the states and a history of commands holds the closures: about
200 bytes a step for commands, and for snapshots the part of each state that
changed, about 8 kB a step for a 1,000-row list.

## Announcements

Pressing the key gives a screen reader nothing to hear on its own. Pass
`announce` and the history calls it after every undo and redo with a message
such as "Undid move Ada up". Send it to a live region, or to the shared
announcer.

```js
const history = createHistory({
  announce: (message) => announce(message, { channel: "history" }),
  undoneText: (label) => `Undid ${label ?? "the last change"}`,
  redoneText: (label) => `Redid ${label ?? "the last change"}`,
});
```

The history keeps no language of its own beyond the two English defaults, so
pass translated functions to `undoneText` and `redoneText`.

## Server rendering

Both are safe to import and call on the server. `createHistory` is plain data
and `svelte/store`, and an action never runs there. A history made at module
level is shared by every request, so make one per component instance.
